WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.424
Module 5: from evidence to findings.

00:00:02.424 --> 00:00:08.081
Northwind now has seven submitted syntheses
and three hundred and fifty-six stories.

00:00:08.081 --> 00:00:13.333
In about fifty minutes, you'll do the
program lead's real work: check what the

00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:17.306
figures can carry, read the
two streams against each other,

00:00:17.306 --> 00:00:21.211
and turn an AI draft into a
report you'd put your name to.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.010
The steps run in a set order,
because each one feeds the next.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.930
Close collection on the day you announced.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.128
Move to Phase 4 once
every team has submitted.

00:00:32.128 --> 00:00:36.021
Then save the story patterns,
which computes the scores.

00:00:36.021 --> 00:00:37.968
Approve the story summaries.

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:39.637
Only then press Analyze.

00:00:39.637 --> 00:00:43.669
Edit every section, curate the
quotes, and save as you go,

00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:47.423
because the report builder
doesn't save automatically.

00:00:47.423 --> 00:00:50.760
Move to In review, then
Final, and save the PDF.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.568
Why does the order matter?

00:00:52.568 --> 00:00:56.669
Analyze reads the saved figures
and the approved summaries.

00:00:56.669 --> 00:01:01.119
Run it before they exist, and the
draft is written without them.

00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:07.229
Before anything is saved, leads and
facilitators can already see the figures

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:10.837
derived from the stories,
marked "not saved yet".

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:15.917
Press Fill from stories, and the
form fills itself, domain by domain.

00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:17.095
It doesn't save.

00:01:17.095 --> 00:01:21.880
A domain is only ticked if all
three question types have answers.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.709
Review the figures, each
with the stories behind it.

00:01:25.709 --> 00:01:31.746
Avoid typing over a figure: a typed-in
number loses its story count and its margin

00:01:31.746 --> 00:01:35.354
of error, and the report
can't place that domain.

00:01:35.354 --> 00:01:36.532
Then press Save.

00:01:36.532 --> 00:01:42.275
Saving computes the composites, and every
change is recorded with who made it.

00:01:42.275 --> 00:01:44.631
Only a Program Lead can do this.

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.049
Here are Northwind's wave one patterns.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:53.806
Stakeholder Relations is highest, at
sixty-six, plus or minus five point two.

00:01:53.806 --> 00:01:59.190
HR Systems is lowest, at forty point
six, plus or minus five point four.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.732
And Artifacts isn't scored at all.

00:02:01.732 --> 00:02:03.451
Keep this exhibit open.

00:02:03.451 --> 00:02:06.293
We'll use it for the next few screens.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.637
Eleven people told an Artifacts story.

00:02:09.637 --> 00:02:11.801
So why does it say only nine?

00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:15.459
Because a domain is judged
by its thinnest input.

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:21.580
All eleven answered the sliders and the
triangle, but only nine placed any stones.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.910
A composite is only as
well-evidenced as its weakest part,

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.314
so Artifacts counts as nine,
and below ten it isn't scored.

00:02:30.314 --> 00:02:35.241
The same floor stops quotes, dots
and percentages for that domain.

00:02:35.241 --> 00:02:38.227
Six dots can be a picture of six people.

00:02:38.227 --> 00:02:41.885
The report lists Artifacts
under "Not yet heard",

00:02:41.885 --> 00:02:44.721
and says the action is more listening.

00:02:44.721 --> 00:02:49.275
So the Artifacts finding rests
on the team's fieldwork alone.

00:02:49.275 --> 00:02:49.798
Say so.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.531
When you glance at a score table,

00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:57.398
the overlap rule keeps you safe: if
two ranges overlap, don't rank them.

00:02:57.398 --> 00:03:01.589
But the report's "Where to start"
section uses a sharper test.

00:03:01.589 --> 00:03:05.982
It takes the two margins and
combines them: square each one, add,

00:03:05.982 --> 00:03:07.402
take the square root.

00:03:07.402 --> 00:03:11.999
Then it asks whether the gap between
the scores is bigger than that.

00:03:11.999 --> 00:03:16.595
Because that combined bar is smaller
than simply adding the margins,

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:20.583
two domains can be told apart
even when their ranges touch.

00:03:20.583 --> 00:03:22.949
Then the report groups the domains.

00:03:22.949 --> 00:03:28.222
Everything it can't tell apart from the
lowest goes in Start here, as a group,

00:03:28.222 --> 00:03:29.236
never in order.

00:03:29.236 --> 00:03:33.562
Everything it can't tell apart
from the highest goes in Protect.

00:03:33.562 --> 00:03:37.347
And domains without enough
stories are named separately,

00:03:37.347 --> 00:03:41.944
because not hearing about something
isn't the same as it being fine.

00:03:42.433 --> 00:03:43.283
Now try it.

00:03:43.283 --> 00:03:47.529
Using Northwind's scores,
work out three combined bars,

00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:52.547
then sort the domains into Start
here, Protect and Not yet heard.

00:03:52.547 --> 00:03:54.554
A calculator is on screen.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:58.278
You know a composite is
a pattern, not a grade.

00:03:58.278 --> 00:04:03.880
Even two domains in the middle of the table
can be telling very different stories.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:09.141
Values' stones lean healthy, and where
they drain, it's the predictable kind.

00:04:09.141 --> 00:04:12.899
Where History's aren't healthy,
they're mostly chaotic,

00:04:12.899 --> 00:04:16.179
its triangle leans towards
getting the job done,

00:04:16.179 --> 00:04:19.322
and fewer people say they
knew what to expect.

00:04:19.322 --> 00:04:21.235
Values reads as predictable.

00:04:21.235 --> 00:04:25.676
History reads as change landing
in ways people didn't see coming.

00:04:25.676 --> 00:04:31.142
Before you write any finding, read the
quadrants, the triangle and both sliders,

00:04:31.142 --> 00:04:32.918
then the team's synthesis.

00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:37.918
For each prompt, an AI summary
sorts the stories into a few kinds,

00:04:37.918 --> 00:04:39.549
with a count and a tone.

00:04:39.549 --> 00:04:42.539
Three things to know
before you approve one.

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:46.956
The AI sorts, but the code counts,
and you can't edit the counts.

00:04:46.956 --> 00:04:51.102
Tone is the AI's reading of the
words, not the storyteller's.

00:04:51.102 --> 00:04:54.499
And the product checks the
two against each other.

00:04:54.499 --> 00:04:56.878
Look at Northwind's Rituals prompt.

00:04:56.878 --> 00:05:02.586
Nine stories were about huddles squeezed by
short staffing, mostly read as negative.

00:05:02.586 --> 00:05:05.780
But of twenty-three
stories with stones placed,

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.216
six that read as negative were placed as
energising by the people who told them.

00:05:11.216 --> 00:05:14.410
That's over a quarter,
so the summary flags it.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.284
People were frustrated about
losing something they value.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:21.681
Never settle that kind of
gap in favour of the AI.

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:24.020
Here's the heart of the method.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:28.682
Northwind's History team concluded that
the merger is settled history for most

00:05:28.682 --> 00:05:29.041
staff.

00:05:29.041 --> 00:05:33.883
But over half the stories answering the
change prompt describe change through the

00:05:33.883 --> 00:05:35.018
lens of the merger.

00:05:35.018 --> 00:05:39.980
Write two or three sentences for the History
finding, and what you'd tell the team.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.072
Then compare with the model answer.

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:46.595
Analyze drafts the executive
summary, and for each domain,

00:05:46.595 --> 00:05:49.443
findings and two to four recommendations.

00:05:49.443 --> 00:05:54.236
It reads every team's work, marking
unsubmitted teams as provisional.

00:05:54.236 --> 00:05:59.863
It reads the saved figures, the approved
summaries and any linked Culture Mosaic.

00:05:59.863 --> 00:06:02.988
And for each domain, it
reads twelve stories,

00:06:02.988 --> 00:06:05.975
spread evenly across
the collection window.

00:06:05.975 --> 00:06:10.907
The scores use every story, but the
draft's prose rests on that sample,

00:06:10.907 --> 00:06:13.199
and the methodology page says so.

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:18.270
It's told never to name anyone, never
to quote, never to invent evidence,

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:22.229
and never to compare the
survey's numbers with the dig's.

00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:25.286
It never overwrites a
section you've edited.

00:06:25.286 --> 00:06:28.273
The draft replaces the
labour of synthesis,

00:06:28.273 --> 00:06:31.468
not your judgment about
what to tell a client.

00:06:31.967 --> 00:06:34.525
Read every section as the client will.

00:06:34.525 --> 00:06:37.286
The first sentence should be the finding.

00:06:37.286 --> 00:06:39.238
Every claim names its source.

00:06:39.238 --> 00:06:41.999
Disagreement is named, not smoothed over.

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:45.971
And with a linked Culture
Mosaic, compare words with words.

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:50.617
Northwind's patient stories bear
out the survey's Customer statement,

00:06:50.617 --> 00:06:55.936
while stories about referring doctors
and ambulance crews hearing about changes

00:06:55.936 --> 00:06:57.686
late partly contradict it.

00:06:57.686 --> 00:07:03.342
Both belong in the finding, and neither
involves setting one number against another.

00:07:03.342 --> 00:07:04.958
No causes you can't see.

00:07:04.958 --> 00:07:10.277
Describe what the culture does and what
it costs, not whether it's good or bad.

00:07:10.277 --> 00:07:13.441
Make recommendations
specific to this evidence.

00:07:13.441 --> 00:07:18.491
Then add sections that cross domains,
and write the executive summary last.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:23.427
Nothing the AI suggests as a
quote prints until you approve it.

00:07:23.427 --> 00:07:25.745
Before you do, check four things.

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:27.151
Is it word for word?

00:07:27.151 --> 00:07:29.259
You may cut, but never reword.

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:32.913
Does the domain have at
least ten stories this wave?

00:07:32.913 --> 00:07:38.393
Could it identify anyone, through a unit,
a shift, a rare role or an incident?

00:07:38.393 --> 00:07:43.242
And is it typical of the pattern,
rather than the most vivid outlier?

00:07:43.733 --> 00:07:47.376
Draft while you work, In
review while colleagues read,

00:07:47.376 --> 00:07:49.400
and Final when it's delivered.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.705
Every move into Final
stores a delivered version,

00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:55.470
with the scores and margins as they were,

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:59.248
so you can always show exactly
what the client received.

00:07:59.248 --> 00:08:02.688
Before you send the PDF,
read the methodology page.

00:08:02.688 --> 00:08:07.208
It says the stories are self-selected,
not a representative sample.

00:08:07.208 --> 00:08:11.862
The scores compare within this
program, not with other organizations,

00:08:11.862 --> 00:08:14.965
and there's no external
benchmark behind them.

00:08:14.965 --> 00:08:18.000
And the margin of error
covers sampling only.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.226
Your readout should say the same.

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:25.391
Story patterns has two downloads:
a scores file and a stories file.

00:08:25.391 --> 00:08:28.311
The stories file warns, in its own header,

00:08:28.311 --> 00:08:31.856
that unattributed isn't
the same as unidentifiable.

00:08:31.856 --> 00:08:37.001
Keep it off shared drives, delete
working copies when the report is final,

00:08:37.001 --> 00:08:42.075
and don't paste stories into another
tool without the client's agreement.

00:08:42.567 --> 00:08:46.573
A second wave answers one
question: did anything move?

00:08:46.573 --> 00:08:49.393
The report uses the same combined bar.

00:08:49.393 --> 00:08:53.993
In a hypothetical wave two, if
Rituals rose by nine point one,

00:08:53.993 --> 00:08:58.667
against a bar of seven point
seven, that's an established rise,

00:08:58.667 --> 00:09:00.967
even though the ranges overlap.

00:09:00.967 --> 00:09:05.493
If HR Systems rose by three
point four, against the same bar,

00:09:05.493 --> 00:09:07.793
it's not an established change.

00:09:07.793 --> 00:09:12.839
It's the same organization measured
twice, not a slight improvement.

00:09:12.839 --> 00:09:16.771
And a domain typed in by
hand can't be tested at all.

00:09:16.771 --> 00:09:22.929
In the app, the Mosaic Signal tab's change
across waves chart draws a line for each

00:09:22.929 --> 00:09:23.448
domain.

00:09:23.448 --> 00:09:26.045
A line that climbs isn't a verdict.

00:09:26.045 --> 00:09:30.052
The verdict is in the report's
"what changed" section.

00:09:30.052 --> 00:09:33.910
To keep waves comparable,
reuse last wave's prompts,

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:37.620
pair any reworded prompt
with its earlier version,

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.107
and keep the follow-up
questions word for word.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.198
Time for the module check: eight
questions, and you need seven right.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.135
Here's your Synthesis
and Report QA Checklist,

00:09:50.135 --> 00:09:54.242
from saving the figures to
the last check before Final.

00:09:54.242 --> 00:09:55.138
Download it.

00:09:55.633 --> 00:09:56.346
To sum up.

00:09:56.346 --> 00:10:00.762
Save the figures and approve the
summaries before you analyze.

00:10:00.762 --> 00:10:03.683
A domain is judged by its thinnest input.

00:10:03.683 --> 00:10:08.741
"Where to start" uses the combined
bar, and names groups, never orders.

00:10:08.741 --> 00:10:10.593
Disagreement is a finding.

00:10:10.593 --> 00:10:14.938
Analyze drafts from a sample
and never overwrites your edits.

00:10:14.938 --> 00:10:17.859
And Final keeps what the client received.

00:10:17.859 --> 00:10:19.640
Next: the practical task.
